  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Another shortwave is expected to move through this \nafternoon, and is currently centered over the St. Lawrence River. As \nopposed to yesterdays forecast, trends have decreased for any wider \nshower and thunderstorm coverage through this afternoon. Subsidence \nin the wake of convection from last night and seasonably cool \ntemperatures in the low to mid 70s has capped much of the region \nthis morning, despite 1500 J/kg of analyzed SBCAPE. To the \nnortheast, thunderstorms have begun to fire in eastern Quebec where \nthe instability gradient is maximized. This is further evident on \nthe visible satellite where the most agitated cumulus are focused \nover the Northeast Kingdom, with lesser vertical motion evident \nacross northern New York. Closer to home here, GOES-19 satellite \ndepicts some dry air aloft in the 500-300mb layer with little \nimprovement depicted upstream. Models have picked up this weaker \nshower trend quite nicely in the HRRR. Showers will mainly be \nfocused over the Northeast Kingdom through this afternoon, with \nbetter chances closer toward 8 PM. the Storm Prediction Center has \nplaced the NEK in a Marginal Risk for Severe Storms (level 1 out of \n5) for this afternoon and evening. Some small hail to half an inch \nand briefly gusty winds up to 40 MPH may be possible under any \nstronger storms. Though the confidence in any widespread storms is \nlow. Elsewhere an isolated terrain driven shower may be possible, in \naddition to a few isolated showers along the western lake breeze of \nLake Champlain. Most locations today look to remain on the dry side. \nAn isolated rumble of thunder in the Champlain Valley or higher \nterrain cannot be ruled out with decent instability in place, should \na shower grow tall enough to break through the subsidence inversion. \nShower chances will once again decrease after sunset with the loss \nof diurnal heating. \n\nTroughing will slide east with surface high pressure building in by \nFriday. More calm and quieter conditions are expected through \nSaturday. Temperatures will be relatively seasonable in the mid to \nupper 70s. A radiational cooling night is increasingly likely Friday \nnight with lows potentially dipping to the upper 40s to low to mid \n50s. A few pocket low 40s may be possible in the decoupled protected \nvalleys of the Adirondacks and eastern Greens. Saturday looks to be \nthe best of the weekend with temperatures in the low to mid 70s, and \nmountain temperatures in the low to mid 60s. It will be a great day \nfor outdoor activities with plenty of sun and little in the way of \nwind. Shower chances return by Sunday as weak northern and southern \nstream energies interact over the North Country. There is still some \nquestions lingering on how much of an interaction there will be, \nwith some model solutions depicting the southern stream breaking \ndown and remaining south for a potentially drier Sunday. There does \nlook to be a weak cold front associated with the northern stream of \nenergy that has better confidence in at least some shower activity \nby Sunday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Large scale troughing will dominate next week, leading \nto seasonable temperatures and a few shower chances. Humidity will \nalso be on the lower side, with dew points generally in the 50s and \nlow 60s. After any lingering showers exit Monday, the next chance of \nrain does not look to be until late Wednesday from a cold front, and \nthen over the weekend from another potential low pressure system. \nHighs look to be in the 70s and low 80s, and lows look to be in the \n50s in most places. The coldest areas should see some temperatures \nin the 40s and the warmest places, notably along Lake Champlain, \nshould see some lows in the 60s. Overall, it looks to be a pleasant \nand relatively uneventful stretch of summer weather.",
